    "created_at": "2025-10-07T20:35:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-10-07T20:35:48Z",
    "body": "I dug into this some more and I figured out what I'm seeing here. It's not the internal type that's being cloned, it's the [already started](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#already-started) boolean, which is per spec via \"The [cloning steps](https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-node-clone-ext) for [script](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#the-script-element) elements given node, copy, and subtree are to set copy's [already started](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#already-started) to node's [already started](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#already-started).\"\n\nI was able to verify this by leaving the contents of the `<script>` tag empty, which doesn't set the \"already started\" boolean to true ([this example](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#script-processing-model) was super helpful!). Then, inserting and removing will behave as expected when \"type\" changes, allowing something that was inserted as a classic script (but not started) to be turned into an import map by changing `type` and `innerText`. `cloneNode` also behaves per spec as long as I account for \"already started\" status being copied into the clone.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
